WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.982
This table shows why storage alone is not enough.

00:00:02.982 --> 00:00:07.427
ZIP codes may look numeric, but arithmetic on them is meaningless.

00:00:07.427 --> 00:00:11.577
Satisfaction ratings use numbers but represent ordered categories.

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:14.102
Emergency visits are true counts.

00:00:14.102 --> 00:00:19.197
Celsius temperature is a numeric measurement, but its zero point is arbitrary.

00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:24.317
When you see a numeric-looking column, first ask what the numbers mean in the real world.

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:28.862
Only after that should you choose summaries, encodings, or models.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:35.352
Connect this idea to a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity.

00:00:35.352 --> 00:00:40.587
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:40.587 --> 00:00:47.447
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:52.912
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:52.912 --> 00:00:57.840
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
